WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.060
Thanks for tuning into SpaceX Daily, episode 29, it's July 11th, 2026, and I'm Patrick

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:06.820
in Vancouver.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.920
Plenty moving across Starship, Falcon, and Starlink today.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:18.340
Booster 20's record duration static fire moves Starship flight 13 to a July 15 target, and

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.540
tests the thermal margins needed for rapid booster reuse.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:28.820
Booster 20 completed a record duration static fire of all 33 Raptor engines, clearing the

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.220
vehicle for the next integrated flight test.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:38.140
SpaceX now lists July 15 as the target date for flight 13, which will attempt the first

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.280
full booster catch attempt with the updated pin design.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:47.380
The test also exercised the new flame trench and water day loose system at pad 2.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.700
Engineers will review engine performance data and heat shield telemetry before the FAA issues

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.080
the final launch license.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.940
Next milestone watchers are tracking is the first hot fire of ship 39 on the Orbital

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.300
Refueling Test article.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.360
This static fire result directly advances the open question of full rapid reuse cadence

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.720
by confirming longer engine run capability on the booster.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.360
The same test also proved the supporting ground systems.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.600
The day loose system at pad 2 was run at 650,000 gallons per minute.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.060
To confirm, it can suppress the acoustic and thermal loads from a full 33 engine ignition.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:32.420
High speed imagery showed the water curtain fully enveloping the trench and tower base.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.280
The test validates the design change from the earlier single pad configuration.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.360
Engineers will compare acoustic sensor readings against predictions before certifying the pad

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.480
for flight 13.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.140
Launch infrastructure readiness at star base continues to be tracked as a pacing item for

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.200
higher flight rates.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.580
And today's full flow result directly addresses the open question of pad turnaround time between

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.160
successive Starship missions.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.020
The physics behind that water flow is worth a closer look.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:09.740
From an engineering standpoint, the 650,000 gallon per minute water day loose test at pad

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:16.340
2 addresses a fundamental acoustic and thermal problem where the energy released by 33 Raptor

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:22.240
engines at ignition must be absorbed or redirected before it reflects back into the vehicle or erodes

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.160
the launch mount.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.860
Raw water flow rate is the simplest lever because each additional gallon per minute increases

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:34.520
the mass of liquid that can be vaporized, carrying heat away as latent heat of vaporization

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.820
rather than allowing it to couple into steel or concrete.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:44.240
The idiot index here is the ratio of finished day loose system cost to the cost of the water

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.320
and pumps alone.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:51.820
And the gap exists because the nozzles, valves and trench geometry must survive repeated thermal

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.640
shock and debris impact while maintaining precise spray patterns.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.780
By validating the full flow curtain, SpaceX collapses the uncertainty around pad turnaround

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:07.720
time, moving the program one step closer to the rapid reuse cadence required for orbital

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:08.760
refueling flights.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.620
The same physics that protects the tower also sets the minimum water volume needed for a

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:19.940
Florida pad, where salt air corrosion adds another constraint on material choices.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:25.280
High speed camera data from the test will be compared against computational fluid dynamics

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.000
models that predict droplet break up and steam generation rates inside the flame trench.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.140
Those models in turn determine how much margin remains if a future booster returns with a higher

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.680
propellant load or a slightly different engine ignition sequence.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:46.240
The deluge design therefore functions as both a protective system and a data collection

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.400
instrument that feeds directly into the next iteration of pad hardware.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:56.940
SpaceX filed with the FCC to expand the constellation to 100,000 Gen3 satellites,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.800
citing the need for higher laser link density and direct to cell capacity.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:06.500
The application requests new orbital shells and updated spectrum rights.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:12.160
Regulators will evaluate interference and debris mitigation plans before any decision.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:18.540
The filing also references increased backhaul demand from future AI satellite payloads.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.240
Starlink constellation growth continues to be the dominant driver of revenue,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.020
and this filing directly informs the open questions of subscriber growth,

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.300
direct to D cell ramp, and profitability at scale.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:39.660
Booster B1071 flew its 35th mission, deploying 24 Starlink satellites from Vandenberg and extending

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:45.120
the fleet's reuse record. The first stage landing on the drone ship occurred eight minutes,

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.900
20 seconds after liftoff, with all engines performing within expected parameters.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.760
This flight brings the booster within one mission of the previous record holder.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:01.000
SpaceX continues to track cumulative engine starts and grid fins cycles to set life limits.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:09.120
Falcon 9 reuse operations remain at record cadence, and B1071's continued flights keep pressure

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.060
on the open question of annual launch rate ceiling and individual booster life limits.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:21.000
A Falcon 9 launch from Vandenberg is scheduled for 8 p.m. Pacific daylight time and will be visible

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:27.880
across parts of Arizona as a bright ascending streak. Observers are advised to look northwest shortly

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:33.400
after sunset. The mission carries 24 Starlink satellites into a sun synchronous orbit.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:38.980
Falcon 9 cadence from the west coast continues to support the broader launch rate goals,

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:45.020
and tonight's flight adds another data point on how frequently the Vandenberg site can cycle vehicles.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:52.100
Ground crews at Starbase are installing additional ground support equipment ahead of the July 15 target.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:58.760
The company continues site work at Cape Canaveral's launch complex 37 to support future Starship

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:04.280
flights from Florida. Orbital refueling hardware remains in the critical path for the next

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:10.400
demonstration flight. Starship infrastructure development now spans both Texas and Florida sites,

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:16.660
and progress at LC-37 directly addresses the open question of how quickly the program can move

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:23.520
from single pad to multi-site operations. The V3 booster configuration incorporates revised

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:30.500
tank domes and a lighter interstage that reduces dry mass by several tons. Post static fire inspections

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:36.840
will focus on weld integrity and engine bay insulation performance. Data from this test will set the

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:42.940
baseline for the first tower catch attempt. The lighter structural changes on V3 continue the

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:48.800
iterative path toward the mass targets required for full booster recovery and reuse on every flight.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:55.200
SpaceX released new space to see footage showing upgraded buoy and drone coverage for capturing

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:02.020
Starship reentry imagery, including 3D heat shield modeling. The footage highlights how recovery

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:07.860
assets have evolved from early makeshift platforms to integrated tamara arrays that can reconstruct

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:15.100
tile performance after atmospheric entry. Observers on the SpaceX subreddit are discussing the newly

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:20.920
posted official video titled Critical Path that details recent Starship manufacturing changes.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.140
The video walks through tooling updates inside the production building and how those changes

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:32.780
affect ship-to-ship refueling hardware timelines. One thing worth watching is that Chinese state media

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:38.900
released video of a state-owned company's first successful recovery of a reusable rocket booster on

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:45.180
an offshore platform. The landing used a different geometry than SpaceX's drone ship approach and

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:51.600
avoided any tower catch hardware. Analysts note the demonstration shortens the timeline for China to

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:58.140
field an operational reusable medium lift vehicle. Resolution depends on whether follow-on flights

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.400
achieve rapid turnaround and payload to orbit performance comparable to Falcon 9.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:10.540
On the AI front, GROC 4.5 launched with budget tier pricing and direct integration into the

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:17.200
cursor code editor, expanding developer access to the model. The release includes updated inference

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:24.420
optimizations that lower per token cost for routine coding tasks. Cursor is separately developing

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:31.620
sand. A general purpose AI agent intended to compete with chat GPT and clawed in workplace tasks.

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:38.220
The agent focuses on multi-step code refactoring and repository scale edits that mirror workflows

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:45.760
already used inside XAI and SpaceX engineering teams. These releases root new GROC capability

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:53.340
directly to the developer tools used by SpaceX and XAI engineering teams. And a quick market note,

00:08:53.340 --> 00:09:04.200
SPCX closes at $145.30, down 4.6%. Before we go, Starship's next data point arrives on the 15th.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:13.500
That's your SpaceX news for today. SPCX is trading at $145.30, down 4.6%.

